---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { buildPrompt, promptVersion } from '../../judge/prompt.ts'
import { definitions, horizon } from '../data/definitions.ts'
import { judgeRuns } from '../data/judgments.ts'
import { latestJudgeDate, measuredFactorCount, verdict } from '../data/site.ts'

const prompt = buildPrompt()
---

<Base title="Methodology" description="How the P(doom) dashboard defines doom, collapse and abundance, scores its factors, and turns them into judged probabilities." active="methodology">
  <article class="prose-site max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold" style="color: var(--ink)">Methodology</h1>
    <p>
      The site has three layers. <strong>Factors</strong> are measured by others and cited. <strong>Indices</strong> are
      mechanical averages of factor scores. <strong>Probabilities</strong> are judgments made from the factors, under
      fixed definitions. Each layer is labelled, so a measured number never passes for a guess, or the reverse.
    </p>

    <h2 id="definitions">Definitions</h2>
    <p>Every probability refers to the end of {horizon}. The definitions never change when the evidence does.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The five essentials.</strong> {definitions.essentials}</li>
      <li><strong>Abundance.</strong> {definitions.abundance}</li>
      <li><strong>Collapse.</strong> {definitions.collapse}</li>
      <li><strong>Doom.</strong> {definitions.doom}</li>
      <li><strong>Muddling through.</strong> {definitions.muddle}</li>
      <li><strong>Superintelligence.</strong> {definitions.superintelligence}</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Abundance, muddling through and collapse are exclusive and add up to 100% for each country. Doom is global, so it
      also counts as collapse for every country. That is why no country's P(collapse) can be lower than P(doom).
    </p>

    <h2>Factors and scores</h2>
    <p>
      Each factor has a <em>good</em> anchor that scores 0 and a <em>bad</em> anchor that scores 100, with values clamped
      outside that range. Some use a log scale (prices, warheads, income), where each tenfold change moves the score
      the same amount. Fertility is two-sided: falling far below or rising far above 2.1 births per woman both add
      pressure. Where a published threshold exists, such as a planetary boundary or WRI's “extremely high” water stress,
      it is drawn as a tick and marked <em>published</em>. All other anchors are marked <em>judgment</em>, with a reason,
      on the <a class="link" href="/factors/">factors page</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Factors are measured, not estimated. When a source has no value, the cell stays empty and the factor drops out of
      that country's indices; nothing is filled in.
    </p>

    <h2>Indices</h2>
    <p>
      Like the composite indices on AI leaderboards, each index averages its benchmark scores, here with equal weights:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pressure index:</strong> the average of the drivers pushing toward collapse.</li>
      <li><strong>Resilience index:</strong> the average strength of the counterforces.</li>
      <li><strong>Essentials gap:</strong> the average pressure on the five essentials, plus a count of how many already reach the abundance threshold.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      An index of 70 does not mean a 70% chance of anything. It is a summary of where a country stands. Turning
      that into a probability takes a model or a judgment, which is the next layer.
    </p>

    <h2>Probabilities: an AI judge, run several times</h2>
    <p>
      Nobody has observed a civilization collapsing in a world with superintelligence, so no dataset can calibrate these
      probabilities. Instead, a frontier model acts as the judge. It receives the fixed definitions and every factor value
      above, then answers in a strict JSON format. The prompt is published below.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Current judge: {verdict.model}, {verdict.runs} independent runs, last on {latestJudgeDate}. The headline is the median of each value; the spread is shown next to it.</li>
      <li>Every run is checked for coherence before it reaches the site. Probabilities stay in [0, 1], abundance and collapse cannot add up to more than 1, a total is at least its largest cause and at most their sum, and P(SI by 2050) ≤ P(SI by 2100). A run that breaks a rule is rejected.</li>
      <li>The judge gives each value twice, with and without superintelligence. The headline combines them by the law of total probability: P(X) = P(SI) × P(X | SI) + (1 − P(SI)) × P(X | no SI).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Language models are not calibrated forecasters. They lean toward numbers common in their training data, and
      they are sensitive to wording. The defences are a fixed, published prompt, several runs, and every input on the
      page. A disagreement can always be traced to a number or a definition.
    </p>

    <h2>What this does not do yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No backtest.</strong> Countries have collapsed before (Somalia, Syria, Lebanon, Venezuela). The next step is to score the factors as they stood in 2000 and check how well the indices predicted collapses by 2020. That would give country probabilities a calibrated anchor.</li>
      <li><strong>One judge model.</strong> Running other frontier models and a superforecaster panel through the same prompt would show how much the answer depends on the judge.</li>
      <li><strong>Hand-collected data.</strong> {measuredFactorCount} factors were collected in a November 2026 research pass. A scheduled pipeline should refresh them from their sources.</li>
      <li><strong>Equal weights.</strong> Every factor counts the same in its index. Letting readers choose weights or presets is planned.</li>
      <li><strong>Eight countries.</strong> The set was chosen for contrast, not coverage.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The judge prompt (version {promptVersion})</h2>
    <p>This is the exact text sent to every run, generated from the site's own data files.</p>
    <pre>{prompt}</pre>

    <h2>Run log</h2>
    <ul>
      {judgeRuns.map((run) => <li>{run.id}: {run.model}, {run.date}, {run.method}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
